Setup Your Raspberry Pi or Linux System for that Project

This section counts for 30% of your final project grade. Your ability to get your project working will require you to use all the skills you've learned in the class and may require you to figure some aspects out on your own. This is a great way to learn more about Linux and your system!

You will need to follow your specific project's steps to get your system working. This may involve changing the OS, or installing and configuring new software, or adding new hardware. What ever your project is, there should be steps that you can find and follow, or you may have to figure some aspects out on your own. If you have any questions along the way, feel free to reach out to me and I'll help as best I can, but please be aware that I have not done all of the various projects out there and may not have experience with your particular issue.

Demonstrate that the System Works, Enhancing it if Desired (Extra Credit)

This section counts for 10% of your final project grade, and you have the chance to earn up to 10% extra credit if you enhance your system beyond the original project. For example, if your project involves getting your Pi or Linux system to play classic games, you could enhance your system by figuring out how to automatically have your Pi or Linux system boot directly into the gaming system. This might earn you 5% extra credit (auto-login and auto-run of applications is fairly easy to do).

You can take a video, pictures, screenshots, or whatever will show me that your system is running the project as expected. For example, if your project involves getting your Pi or Linux system to play classic games, you should have a video, picture, or screenshot showing your system playing a game.

Report on Your Project through a Written Paper, Presentation, Video, or Demonstration

This section counts for 60% of your final project grade. I am allowing you to pick the format of your final report to give you flexibility and to let you to choose the area you're strongest in. If you have any questions about what may be acceptable to do for the report, please ask me.

If you want to come to campus to demonstrate your project to me (still addressing all areas below), please schedule a block of time with me through the Microsoft Bookings tool. Select the "Raspberry Pi" service and find a time that works for you to come to campus to demonstrate your project. NOTE: You will need to arrive before that time to setup your demonstration. I will expect you to start your demonstration at the time you schedule. If you choose to do a demonstration, we will coordinate all of this together.

I want you to address each of the areas listed below. Please do not copy and paste the questions directly into your report. Rather, I want you to make sure you address each of these areas in whatever you produce.

Introduce your project and explain what it's supposed to do. (5% of this section's grade)
